 /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/AYbBHZ7iwi3t2pcm4xCuXrWw9xY3c9k7se6ge7cCTpWaYxx8CuWDkc31Gq-zE8HrszsrfmkjzHut5USVdhoMC8YsK9oe2hcP21onDF-I3iSXtb2tzh25MgukpXq9JEyrGwq1nL9c-330x211.png 330w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-63.png 646w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/AYbBHZ7iwi3t2pcm4xCuXrWw9xY3c9k7se6ge7cCTpWaYxx8CuWDkc31Gq-zE8HrszsrfmkjzHut5USVdhoMC8YsK9oe2hcP21onDF-I3iSXtb2tzh25MgukpXq9JEyrGwq1nL9c-414x265.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 159px) 100vw, 159px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>White\\x3C/b>\\x3Cb>: \\x3C/b>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">simplicity, purity, light, innocence, goodness\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Being the lightest colour, white is regarded as the most complete and pure. Apple is distinguished for its simple white branding techniques creating increased sense of sophistication yet simple-to-use products.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11706 size-full\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/V-LnhyvjxX2uqUPCseIggBVCAaLej9SnosL7B7Suz77xhJzSXGi1Ed89GYdRHwZRCSwg4UbCZwK-1Fw2fF8D2jgka6XpgJ18rAERv4wz9vjsP0PuET1bGLHK9yJJqYwrq5qGKWB-e1521031894713.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"Apple logo\\\" width=\\\"173\\\" height=\\\"161\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Multi-color:\\x3C/b> \\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">multi-channel, positive, playful, bold, boundless, diversity\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Companies with multiple colours suggests that they offer a wide choice of products and services. Google, the classic example of a multi-channel, playful company that communicates it effectively through its use of the multi-color scheme.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11707 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/2qL7WEJLebW7DT0w_jiIwaA4KxlL10YGpuJw-E0b8nFEbXyedKlaii4HG-BqxRQvLacf_Ha939nxt-t8Gz6zCNO3VWKNEVaIZUYOxjURcpEJCh7XWlQGQfutH3N-rT7_lAy0jpjQ.png\\\" alt=\\\"Google logo\\\" width=\\\"181\\\" height=\\\"60\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/2qL7WEJLebW7DT0w_jiIwaA4KxlL10YGpuJw-E0b8nFEbXyedKlaii4HG-BqxRQvLacf_Ha939nxt-t8Gz6zCNO3VWKNEVaIZUYOxjURcpEJCh7XWlQGQfutH3N-rT7_lAy0jpjQ.png 389w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/2qL7WEJLebW7DT0w_jiIwaA4KxlL10YGpuJw-E0b8nFEbXyedKlaii4HG-BqxRQvLacf_Ha939nxt-t8Gz6zCNO3VWKNEVaIZUYOxjURcpEJCh7XWlQGQfutH3N-rT7_lAy0jpjQ-300x99.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/2qL7WEJLebW7DT0w_jiIwaA4KxlL10YGpuJw-E0b8nFEbXyedKlaii4HG-BqxRQvLacf_Ha939nxt-t8Gz6zCNO3VWKNEVaIZUYOxjURcpEJCh7XWlQGQfutH3N-rT7_lAy0jpjQ-330x109.png 330w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 181px) 100vw, 181px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11708\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/EAAiLjtWF0T3inq2RD0nP9MnOjybk1bmM1oBz9-q-mXB6Ji3GJR4-85gZJf1KBjX0uYvj3FbCsba0B4k6El-fS7CewgTPRZM52q1dNUKKf4RMf8ekZp9kOKXPaOlmbgCOPdZxcc2.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"NBC logo\\\" width=\\\"102\\\" height=\\\"60\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11709\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/4yiyTHx5kR5psyThN-5ex9IOFzOabKcJAq4iMC5Q-iHIaIVNvBZFFhqTRFsrNSaNL7glRCpupkKTGVqPOdY2KKCd3mpcfynI3j-5MsbaCmfTX0RT88evFTaabrfXA1Lp_KY9ud1w.png\\\" alt=\\\"Olympics logo\\\" width=\\\"120\\\" height=\\\"60\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/4yiyTHx5kR5psyThN-5ex9IOFzOabKcJAq4iMC5Q-iHIaIVNvBZFFhqTRFsrNSaNL7glRCpupkKTGVqPOdY2KKCd3mpcfynI3j-5MsbaCmfTX0RT88evFTaabrfXA1Lp_KY9ud1w.png 318w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/4yiyTHx5kR5psyThN-5ex9IOFzOabKcJAq4iMC5Q-iHIaIVNvBZFFhqTRFsrNSaNL7glRCpupkKTGVqPOdY2KKCd3mpcfynI3j-5MsbaCmfTX0RT88evFTaabrfXA1Lp_KY9ud1w-300x150.png 300w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 120px) 100vw, 120px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"Von_Restorff_Effect\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>8. The Von Restorff Effect\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Also known as \u201cIsolation Effect\u201d, this principle states that \\x3Cstrong>distinctive items are more likely to be remembered than ordinary items.\\x3C/strong>\u00a0This distinction can be a noticeably different visual, context or experience. To stand out from the rest, your designs should create a memorable experience for your users.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-11677\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/vonrestorff.jpeg\\\" alt=\\\"Von Restorff Effect\\\" width=\\\"358\\\" height=\\\"141\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-6.jpeg 358w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-4.jpeg 300w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-39.jpeg 330w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 358px) 100vw, 358px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Call attention to important design elements and concepts\u00a0to make it easier for your users to process and retain what they see and do. \\x3Cstrong>Emphasize important details by altering light, color, size, image, font, animation, words and sounds.\\x3C/strong>\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_11678\\\" style=\\\"width: 1118px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption alignleft\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-11678\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11678 size-full\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM.png\\\" alt=\\\"Medium Website\\\" width=\\\"1108\\\" height=\\\"551\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM.png 1108w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM-300x149.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM-768x382.png 768w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM-1024x509.png 1024w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM-330x164.png 330w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-56.png 736w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/Screen-Shot-2018-03-14-at-2.07.32-PM-414x206.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 1108px) 100vw, 1108px\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-11678\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>Medium uses a green CTA button that contrasts with the background colors to draw attention to it. (Source: \\x3Ca href=\\\"http://www.medium.com\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">www.medium.com\\x3C/a>) \\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">But keep in mind that the Von Restorff principle must be used in moderation. Trying to make several things stand out can cause your users to become overwhelmed with all that is happening on the screen and lose focus. Remember, less is more.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"Visceral_Reactions\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>9. Visceral Reactions\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Ever come across a website you just don&#8217;t want to leave because of how beautiful it is?\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">This feeling is a visceral reaction. It&#8217;s an instinctive response to a stimulus or experience created by chemical messengers in our brain. Eliciting visceral reactions through your design will keep visitors coming back for more. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cstrong>Users make a split-second decision about how they feel about a particular design as soon as they see it.\\x3C/strong> If they enter a site and the layout is not immediately appealing, they may not hang around for too long.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Acquiring a visceral reaction from your users that generates a positive behavioral reaction in them will help you gain their loyalty and support, which will in turn help develop a better image for your brand. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cstrong>Simple design elements such as fonts, colors, imagery, and icons can be determining factors of the overall feel of the site.\\x3C/strong> People are most comfortable with things they can relate to, so while creating a unique experience keep in mind to maintain an underlying familiarity.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_11680\\\" style=\\\"width: 967px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption alignleft\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-11680\\\" class=\\\"size-full wp-image-11680\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL.png\\\" alt=\\\"Airbnb homepage\\\" width=\\\"957\\\" height=\\\"635\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL.png 957w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL-300x199.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL-768x510.png 768w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL-150x100.png 150w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-31.png 330w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/7f0U8ziDfc5Vgb0ETxArnsw8JpBkB2fNFf86qvkVBdmkmGu86MMs6dDJ_60K57zpSKZAowJRPww0w97Et0ji1i_zJ5VhR_WljFsPkfVz7T5JUeG577MEV2TMfrG44oYUuo0N-sBL-624x414.png 624w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-29.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 957px) 100vw, 957px\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-11680\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>Airbnb uses intriguing imagery of destinations to elicit a positive visceral reaction associated with using their website. (Source: \\x3Ca href=\\\"http://www.airbnb.com\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">www.airbnb.com\\x3C/a>)\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"Dual_Coding_Theory\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>10. Dual-Coding Theory\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Make it easier for your users to remember important things from your website by taking advantage of the dual-coding system. The dual-coding theory suggests that memory has two distinct but interconnected systems, one for verbal information and the other for non-verbal information. The relationship between these two channels affects memory and learning. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">You\u00a0create visual images for words in your mind based on your experiences and perception of the real world. Think of the word \u201ctree\u201d your brain automatically imagines a brown trunk and green leaves, or something along those lines. By pairing words with images, it makes them easier to recall upon mentioning them.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">We see this in children\u2019s books all the time. Not only is it for the visual appeal, but it helps children learn new words by imprinting the images associated with them.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_11679\\\" style=\\\"width: 571px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-11679\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11679 size-full\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/mz_POWiHqj9zykha1yzGPQS1jv93Me8EjEMybjtFSCtiqu-gDxWdSKc2OBZiUT1e8bXdiItzlgE4iX3zXmYVFv-7KGHbIPRaa53XGL_YJ8ouTzmv_WsKyMUR50ZfzlFNFla-8xpt.png\\\" alt=\\\"Dual-coding \\\" width=\\\"561\\\" height=\\\"344\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/mz_POWiHqj9zykha1yzGPQS1jv93Me8EjEMybjtFSCtiqu-gDxWdSKc2OBZiUT1e8bXdiItzlgE4iX3zXmYVFv-7KGHbIPRaa53XGL_YJ8ouTzmv_WsKyMUR50ZfzlFNFla-8xpt.png 561w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/mz_POWiHqj9zykha1yzGPQS1jv93Me8EjEMybjtFSCtiqu-gDxWdSKc2OBZiUT1e8bXdiItzlgE4iX3zXmYVFv-7KGHbIPRaa53XGL_YJ8ouTzmv_WsKyMUR50ZfzlFNFla-8xpt-300x184.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/mz_POWiHqj9zykha1yzGPQS1jv93Me8EjEMybjtFSCtiqu-gDxWdSKc2OBZiUT1e8bXdiItzlgE4iX3zXmYVFv-7KGHbIPRaa53XGL_YJ8ouTzmv_WsKyMUR50ZfzlFNFla-8xpt-330x202.png 330w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-22.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 561px) 100vw, 561px\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-11679\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>Dr. Seuss is famous for using the dual coding system in his books for children, making it easier for children to remember what they read.\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Pair an article of text with a visual representation to allow readers to grasp the idea faster, engraving it into their memory. Infographics are a great example of how to make use of the dual-coding system.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Designers need to focus on the design of content itself and not just the layout to both convey messages and ease learnability creating intuitive experiences.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"Psychology_of_Shapes\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>11. The Psychology of Shapes\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cblockquote>\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>&#8220;I found that I could say things with color and shapes that i couldn&#8217;t say any other way&#8221;\u00a0\\x3C/em>&#8211; Georgia O&#8217;Keefe\\x3C/p>\\x3C/blockquote>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Our subconscious mind responds to shapes by associating them to qualities that we think they are representative of.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>We form these connections from universal associations that we encounter without even being consciously aware of it, such as the red octagon that\u00a0is globally-known as the\u00a0STOP sign. Brands use shapes along with colors to form their logos and other visual elements to trigger emotional connections with their brand.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Some commonly used shapes are:\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Circles, Ovals, and Ellipses\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Round shapes create a positive emotional message, perceived feminine trait and suggest community, infinity, relationships and unity.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11686 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/5-pW2I860ZL6muUU41e_ZrrYitP6LYdC6zG_BgyNnIfCegZHS_qMRQV9-6sEQaEgoy-dmP-XCamTddf82orj9IG6FAlzP17TFP7JjghKr8pgDFXNRzLTqB0-HcPORnqa_2nHvuk1.png\\\" alt=\\\"Pepsi Logo\\\" width=\\\"114\\\" height=\\\"155\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/5-pW2I860ZL6muUU41e_ZrrYitP6LYdC6zG_BgyNnIfCegZHS_qMRQV9-6sEQaEgoy-dmP-XCamTddf82orj9IG6FAlzP17TFP7JjghKr8pgDFXNRzLTqB0-HcPORnqa_2nHvuk1.png 193w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/5-pW2I860ZL6muUU41e_ZrrYitP6LYdC6zG_BgyNnIfCegZHS_qMRQV9-6sEQaEgoy-dmP-XCamTddf82orj9IG6FAlzP17TFP7JjghKr8pgDFXNRzLTqB0-HcPORnqa_2nHvuk1-192x260.png 192w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 114px) 100vw, 114px\\\" />\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\" wp-image-11687 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/0lQaJhovYUO039V2iWcqNA8tVk6OIoqsqPVSJwgdDpVhneuf5YQGsQDSGJYjU4zSViJY35X0WnaifJm6Sk5j562N8ULlZlzHsSCKmlWGZ1L3yoEcjWNDh3awV3NbTrGzgbLojJJO.png\\\" alt=\\\"Audi Logo\\\" width=\\\"183\\\" height=\\\"183\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/0lQaJhovYUO039V2iWcqNA8tVk6OIoqsqPVSJwgdDpVhneuf5YQGsQDSGJYjU4zSViJY35X0WnaifJm6Sk5j562N8ULlZlzHsSCKmlWGZ1L3yoEcjWNDh3awV3NbTrGzgbLojJJO.png 225w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/0lQaJhovYUO039V2iWcqNA8tVk6OIoqsqPVSJwgdDpVhneuf5YQGsQDSGJYjU4zSViJY35X0WnaifJm6Sk5j562N8ULlZlzHsSCKmlWGZ1L3yoEcjWNDh3awV3NbTrGzgbLojJJO-150x150.png 150w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/0lQaJhovYUO039V2iWcqNA8tVk6OIoqsqPVSJwgdDpVhneuf5YQGsQDSGJYjU4zSViJY35X0WnaifJm6Sk5j562N8ULlZlzHsSCKmlWGZ1L3yoEcjWNDh3awV3NbTrGzgbLojJJO-200x200.png 200w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 183px) 100vw, 183px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Squares and Triangles\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Logos that have straight edges suggest stability, strength, professionalism and efficiency. Triangles are related to power, and associations with science, religion and law. These shapes are often viewed as a masculine quality.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11688\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/75oWbZPz0fhFYUHJqSvJtlPVkfGbZfL6L7xPIWHbRrW9ApmPOTJfWmYwTkwVG2gpftkNs5oFrzG3K8iWmMnX285NFvJXPgprtUQiuScS7RABZet1zjxFt7LCYjzifJQOK7_v1QQJ.png\\\" alt=\\\"Microsoft Logo\\\" width=\\\"283\\\" height=\\\"60\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/75oWbZPz0fhFYUHJqSvJtlPVkfGbZfL6L7xPIWHbRrW9ApmPOTJfWmYwTkwVG2gpftkNs5oFrzG3K8iWmMnX285NFvJXPgprtUQiuScS7RABZet1zjxFt7LCYjzifJQOK7_v1QQJ.png 486w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/75oWbZPz0fhFYUHJqSvJtlPVkfGbZfL6L7xPIWHbRrW9ApmPOTJfWmYwTkwVG2gpftkNs5oFrzG3K8iWmMnX285NFvJXPgprtUQiuScS7RABZet1zjxFt7LCYjzifJQOK7_v1QQJ-300x64.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/75oWbZPz0fhFYUHJqSvJtlPVkfGbZfL6L7xPIWHbRrW9ApmPOTJfWmYwTkwVG2gpftkNs5oFrzG3K8iWmMnX285NFvJXPgprtUQiuScS7RABZet1zjxFt7LCYjzifJQOK7_v1QQJ-330x70.png 330w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-49.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 283px) 100vw, 283px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11689\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/ZhASSNGdFIQ_JLgnKGRX0ip_2jVB6xq3e0DM5t0qiZUD7jYcYAQZVmEhp3dSa5PWOByoiaQUD5wpY0SCz7LXGQKRNTm1YI6ZV6IA3uFtT9sys380Z0DBmRfeMq-4NtzuOshimtye.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"Google Drive\\\" width=\\\"69\\\" height=\\\"60\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Vertical Lines\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Vertical lines in logos such as Cisco and SoundCloud are associated with masculinity, strength, and aggression. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\" wp-image-11691 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/7-zeNm2rhqCNi_XHZEKNJL8-CfeWwxoWZKYzosvSnToX_sIcIF3qtnu2dyjHb6X3CAafK2S44blTsuBajHtTBK6S5Deb8zTLTm7GHON9MlP1TeXGoUn3wWSNL9W0NuSZ4w0AWGd.png\\\" alt=\\\"Cisco Logo\\\" width=\\\"194\\\" height=\\\"109\\\" />\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11692 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/ul6YNyOwo89R0DYQS2wf2dfx8Fsxu2YGtRFo9YZ6ftQinJX8WdtC84NEc-IZHDEwYdbZ8Y2AvqKtxYBaT_uwhOq2Uhn32g_3G0O0DP9k2bgjmc7oriNktcChZw-UCanSMOVThP54.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"Soundcloud Logo\\\" width=\\\"174\\\" height=\\\"130\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Horizontal Lines\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Horizontal lines in logos such as AT&amp;T and Human Rights Campaign are connected with community, equality, tranquility, and calmness.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\" wp-image-11693 alignleft\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/yNfZokKnNuL9fYfB_OYIlxUhCbW3EfC7BWrZIDoFgQe5RFNti0RrdWP1NhpokzCKzP7M6HgwcW7cqg3TXIQ8UU1979ApN8-uIdJnn0k3J_6T7sHTQ4d6tKYG-wrXlvnoe3cobF2F.png\\\" alt=\\\"AT&amp;T Logo\\\" width=\\\"204\\\" height=\\\"153\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"alignleft wp-image-11694\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/Dzv_2cA5E4PU-5jvmg2-lbcvn6tXz3hxaqnrxXjKAtkogmmxnZy3hDaNmQ9mQj5fzrkekFcX5iAiRIA0n_RWPgVYX4GMFA7SOAoE2k5dWzdVRzWhZg8Xs0b-3zasDTGhZD-7PtRw.png\\\" alt=\\\"Human Rights\\\" width=\\\"327\\\" height=\\\"179\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"Pareto_Principle\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>12. The Pareto Principle, or the 80/20 Rule\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The Pareto Principle, also know as the 80/20 Rule or the Law of the Vital Few, states that just 20% of the work that you have done to achieve something is responsible for 80% of the results that you\u2019ve achieved. \\x3Cstrong>Simply put, you only need to fix a little in order to get a lot of positive change.\\x3C/strong>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"aligncenter wp-image-11681\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/wqfeP_gqxG-dIAdopbe7B37QdCwMx1_t2CMAC8ZLTnvuSZ2zYWddViOPOGYV7i7qNpHqrOO1iZLwaZS8LqzbbHTGVcC0kW5cNGcE7kKcockdV4jdOT40m4qchcyJG3WMU5ajnUxr.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"80/20 Rule\\\" width=\\\"355\\\" height=\\\"259\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-54.jpg 481w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-37.jpg 300w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-14.jpg 330w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-19.jpg 245w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-20.jpg 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 355px) 100vw, 355px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Trying to tackle and perfect all your issues at once is overwhelming and yields diminishing results. Prioritising your efforts on the small portion that could have the biggest impact will definitely help you fare better.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Analyze your design&#8217;s functioning to discover your 80/20 requirements. With fewer elements to focus on, you can maximize precision and finesse to increase conversion rates with minimal wasted efforts.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cstrong>Keep in mind that although you do want to focus on the 20 percent in your design, it does not mean the remaining 80 percent is irrelevant.\\x3C/strong> Where time and resources permit, fine-tune all details to create the ultimate user experience.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca name=\\\"fitts_law\\\">\\x3C/a>\\x3Cb>13. Fitts\u2019 Law\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cstrong>Fitts\u2019 Law proposes that the time taken to move to a target area is a function of the size of the target and distance to the target.\\x3C/strong>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The law suggests placing target buttons closer to expected mouse locations and making them larger to decrease interaction time. This concept is important in web design because the time required to take a desired action affects conversion rates and any additional time is a risk of losing a potential customer.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_11682\\\" style=\\\"width: 449px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\\">\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.interaction-design.org/literature/article/fitts-s-law-the-importance-of-size-and-distance-in-ui-design\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-11682\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-11682\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/J6g7az-rSQjCZHQYkZjY4LJhkDTg1Tj1Kl1ObH4a0xCTIiQC-tsWEEBfWLrZfzVIiVE0z9GDhVBVFiS-E-L-QaGtoFOe8oute7VsT0VY0V9QoWiQivvEYNU0JldeejHRse1OD3Hb.png\\\" alt=\\\"Fitts Law\\\" width=\\\"439\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/J6g7az-rSQjCZHQYkZjY4LJhkDTg1Tj1Kl1ObH4a0xCTIiQC-tsWEEBfWLrZfzVIiVE0z9GDhVBVFiS-E-L-QaGtoFOe8oute7VsT0VY0V9QoWiQivvEYNU0JldeejHRse1OD3Hb.png 539w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/J6g7az-rSQjCZHQYkZjY4LJhkDTg1Tj1Kl1ObH4a0xCTIiQC-tsWEEBfWLrZfzVIiVE0z9GDhVBVFiS-E-L-QaGtoFOe8oute7VsT0VY0V9QoWiQivvEYNU0JldeejHRse1OD3Hb-300x215.png 300w, /uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/inline-0.png 330w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/J6g7az-rSQjCZHQYkZjY4LJhkDTg1Tj1Kl1ObH4a0xCTIiQC-tsWEEBfWLrZfzVIiVE0z9GDhVBVFiS-E-L-QaGtoFOe8oute7VsT0VY0V9QoWiQivvEYNU0JldeejHRse1OD3Hb-414x297.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 439px) 100vw, 439px\\\" />\\x3C/a>\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-11682\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>(Source: www.interaction-design.org)\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Increasing link size when hovering over items, as in Apple Macbook\u2019s menu bar, is useful for increasing usability index. A common misuse of Fitts\u2019 law is when the text of a menu bar is clickable but the tabs themselves are not, decreasing its usability index.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"aligncenter wp-image-11683\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2018/03/et0aurAtGy9DcEVVedSRfFbCDOCrxCykE8NHRsOy_aakreoM9FU2qAtYae-98b5wHGWZdXhiOLiITrtdSgP3ZP5s2tE90fx9Ky05AE4vhn_14jnO8zGRyMTm58JH1CrZQTbfM8Tx.png\\\" alt=\\\"Fitts Law\\\" width=\\\"447\\\" height=\\\"279\\\" srcset=\\\"/wp-contentuploads/2018/03/et0aurAtGy9DcEVVedSRfFbCDOCrxCykE8NHRsOy_aakreoM9FU2qAtYae-98b5wHGWZdXhiOLiITrtdSgP3ZP5s2tE90fx9Ky05AE4vhn_14jnO8zGRyMTm58JH1CrZQTbfM8Tx.png 400w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/et0aurAtGy9DcEVVedSRfFbCDOCrxCykE8NHRsOy_aakreoM9FU2qAtYae-98b5wHGWZdXhiOLiITrtdSgP3ZP5s2tE90fx9Ky05AE4vhn_14jnO8zGRyMTm58JH1CrZQTbfM8Tx-300x188.png 300w, /wp-contentuploads/2018/03/et0aurAtGy9DcEVVedSRfFbCDOCrxCykE8NHRsOy_aakreoM9FU2qAtYae-98b5wHGWZdXhiOLiITrtdSgP3ZP5s2tE90fx9Ky05AE4vhn_14jnO8zGRyMTm58JH1CrZQTbfM8Tx-330x206.png 330w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 447px) 100vw, 447px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The law can also be used for undesired actions, such as delete buttons, decreasing their target size and placing it further away from mouse position reduces the likelihood of making mistakes and frustrating the user.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Conclusion\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Considering psychology in your design process helps fulfill business goals while strengthening user relations. Understanding how a design is perceived and interpreted is a crucial asset that designers must possess. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Follow usability best practices to satisfy usability principles. Enable learnability, efficiency, and memorability of your system. Take advantage of psychological principles to ethically guide user behaviour. Create seamless experiences to boost conversion rates and user satisfaction.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Any successful design will need updating from time to time, but the best part about psychology principles is they\u2019re timeless solutions to your designs.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>At UXBERT Labs we specialise in using scientific UCD processes to design world-class innovative and beautiful experiences. With specialist UX teams in Dubai &amp; Riyadh, we&#8217;ll design and build software that will help your businesses succeed. If you have a project you\u2019d like to work with us on, get in touch at\\x3C/em> hello@uxbert.com\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},excerpt:$R[185]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>In this article we detail 13 psychological principles that UX designers should understand and use when designing any product.\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},author:3,featured_media:37597,comment_status:\"closed\",ping_status:\"closed\",sticky:!1,template:\"\",format:\"standard\",meta:$R[186]={_acf_changed:!1,inline_featured_image:!1,_mi_skip_tracking:!1,footnotes:\"\",_vp_format_video_url:\"\",_vp_image_focal_point:$R[187]=[]},categories:$R[188]=[9,203,12,196],tags:$R[189]=[34,199,86,187,100,104],acf:$R[190]={main:\"/uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/main.png\",sec:\"/uploads/articles/ux-psychology-principles-design-ux/sec.png\"},category_objects:$R[191]=[$R[192]={id:9,count:3,description:\"\",link:\"/category/interaction-design\",name:\"Interaction Design\",slug:\"interaction-design\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[193]=[]},$R[170],$R[102],$R[194]={id:196,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/category/web-development\",name:\"Web Development\",slug:\"web-development\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[195]=[]}],tag_objects:$R[196]=[$R[175],$R[197]={id:199,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/emotional-design\",name:\"emotional design\",slug:\"emotional-design\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[198]=[]},$R[199]={id:86,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/psychology\",name:\"Psychology\",slug:\"psychology\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[200]=[]},$R[201]={id:187,count:1,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/ui-design\",name:\"UI Design\",slug:\"ui-design\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[202]=[]},$R[203]={id:100,count:3,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/usability-guidelines\",name:\"usability guidelines\",slug:\"usability-guidelines\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[204]=[]},$R[205]={id:104,count:5,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/user-experience-design\",name:\"user experience design\",slug:\"user-experience-design\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[206]=[]}]},$R[207]={id:10944,date:\"2018-02-07T10:35:50\",date_gmt:\"2018-02-07T10:35:50\",guid:$R[208]={rendered:\"/?p=10944\"},modified:\"2026-02-19T11:33:17\",modified_gmt:\"2026-02-19T11:33:17\",slug:\"usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-e-commerce-website-souq-com\",status:\"publish\",link:\"/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-e-commerce-website-souq-com\",title:$R[209]={rendered:\"Usability Engineering in Saudi Arabia: E-commerce Website Souq.com\"},content:$R[210]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">In the first of our Usability Engineering series, we ran some quick usability tests on e-services available through the \\x3Ca href=\\\"/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-ministry-of-interior/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">Saudi Ministry of Interior\u2019s website\\x3C/a>. The aim was to show how even the simplest usability tests can bring to light significant usability flaws in your design.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Moving away from government e-services, for our second article in this series we\u2019re looking at usability testing on e-commerce websites.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">E-commerce websites (and apps) are great candidates for usability evaluation as even the smallest design changes based on insights collected from usability testing can result in a significant increase in sales.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The best example of this is the \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://articles.uie.com/three_hund_million_button/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">$300 million button case study by Jared Spool and UIE\\x3C/a>\u00a0where a single design change to the checkout journey based on insights from usability testing\u00a0resulted in an increase in purchases of 45%.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Test Subject: SOUQ.COM\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">For this set of usability test sessions, we asked users to browse, choose and purchase a laptop on the Saudi Souq (\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://saudi.souq.com/sa-ar/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">saudi.souq.com\\x3C/a>) website.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Souq.com is an English and Arabic language e-commerce platform which sells a range of products like consumer electronics, beauty products and household goods. It is the largest e-commerce retailer in the Middle East. Often described as the Amazon of the Arab world, it was actually recently acquired by Amazon.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cbr />\\n\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">A total of 3 users took part in the tests, each of whom were asked to use the site to purchase a laptop of their choice from the website. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Ideal User Journey For Task Success\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cbr />\\n\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cbr />\\n\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The following is a list of the steps the user\u00a0would ideally take to navigate to task success in the quickest, most efficient way.\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cbr />\\n\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cbr />\\n\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Task: \\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Purchasing a laptop\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 1: Hover over the \u201cElectronics\u201d label on the main navigational menu on the Homepage\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 2: Hover over \u201cComputers &amp; Laptops\u201d in the \u201cElectronics\u201d dropdown\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 3: Click \u201cLaptops\u201d\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 4: Navigate to the filter widget and filter by brand, type, screen size etc. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 5: Click on the laptop of your choice\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 6: Click on \u201cADD TO CART\u201d\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 7: Click on \u201cPROCEED TO CHECKOUT\u201d\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 8: Select the available shipping address or add a new one\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 9: Select a shipping service\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 10: Select a payment method\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 11: Enter your payment details \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 12: Click on \u201cPLACE ORDER\u201d\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/AspHUuSA4wI\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Issues and Recommendations\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The issues identified below\u00a0are been listed in the order they took place as users went along their journey\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>1. Users Were Frustrated By The Default Viewing Mode on Product Listing Page\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The product listing page had two viewing modes: a default \u2018List View\u2019 with detailed product descriptions and small images, and a \u2018Grid View\u2019 with larger product images without a detailed description\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Although the user wanted to compare products using their pictures, he did not notice the option for changing the viewing mode to the grid view. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Another user discovered the grid view after browsing products for a long time and said he preferred this view because he could go through all the products faster. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/zWkeQ7DwzvU\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Customers hardly read everything on the page, especially when there is a lot of content. Studies report that about eighty percent of users scan any new page and only sixteen percent read each word. \u00a0We found the same pattern in our testing sessions too. Customers in the list view \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U8HO5S1usyQ\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">did not initially pay attention to the lengthy product description.\\x3C/a>\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Their gaze alternated from the product price to the product image. Only if they were interested in the product did their gaze shift to the product description. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">However, since the users we tested with weren&#8217;t actually planning on buying the laptop, their behaviour isn&#8217;t 100% representative. A user who&#8217;s actually in a &#8216;buying state&#8217; may be more interested in product details (the text) than someone who&#8217;s just &#8216;browsing&#8217;. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">So although the users in this research preferred grid view, further research would be required for a conclusive solution.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.digitalsurgeons.com/thoughts/creative/list-vs-grid-view-how-do-you-choose/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">Many case studies tout one layout over the other, but they aren\u2019t necessarily a definitive source of evidence that there is no one right answer.\\x3C/a> \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">List view provides users a format that follows their natural reading patterns like the F-shaped pattern, while grid view is a little more interruptive, making it best suited for visual content. It all depends on the type of content on your website and the audience you\u2019re catering to. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">In the end, the decision to use list view versus grid view should be aligned with how your users behave. In this study, since participants preferred the easily scannable grid view, making it the default view would be a potential solution. However, more rigorous usability engineering research is required to ensure the fidelity of this solution.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>2. No Filter by Year\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Users wanted to filter products based on the year they were released but the Filter widget did not offer any such option. The only way they could determine the year was through the product description. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/O1Tld3Fjgs0\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Add an option for product release year in the Filter widget. People will do the least amount of work possible to get a task done. To minimize workload, users must always be provided with good ways to reduce available options as efficiently as possible. The system should do as many things as possible, so that users can concentrate on performing tasks that actually require human processing and input.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>3. Items In Saudi Website Cannot Be Shipped To Riyadh\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The homepage of the website enables users to select their shipping location. Although the users selected \u201cShip to Saudi\u201d, many items on the product listing page couldn\u2019t be shipped to Riyadh. This frustrated the user and he considered leaving the website. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Shipping detail issues are common nuisances in Saudi ecommerce websites. In our more formal usability research, we found that e-commerce websites often do not provide sufficient shipping information until the last stages of the checkout process. When users realize that the item doesn\u2019t ship to their location after completing the whole checkout process, they get frustrated and consider leaving the website completely.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://econsultancy.com/blog/10936-site-speed-case-studies-tips-and-tools-for-improving-your-conversion-rate\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">In fact, 88% of online consumers are less likely to ever return to a site after having a single bad experience.\\x3C/a>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/f1NdYi7pYsU\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">One possible solution to this issue is to have a single, consistent website instead of separate ones for each shipping locations. Users should be allowed to filter the products based on the country it ships to on the product listing page. If the user does end up clicking on a product that does not ship to their location, a clear message should be provided in the product description well in advance.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_10950\\\" style=\\\"width: 220px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-10950\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-10950 size-full\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2017/08/shipping-1.png\\\" alt=\\\"Amazon Filter Options\\\" width=\\\"210\\\" height=\\\"225\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-10950\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">Amazon allows users to filter products based on shipping location.\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ci>\\x3C/i>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>4.\u00a0\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>\u2018Check Where It Ships\u2019 Dropdown Menu Listed \u2018Riyadh\u2019 For Items That Don\u2019t Ship to Riyadh.\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">When the user encounters an item which does not ship to Riyadh, the website provides a link for them to \u2018Check Where It Ships\u2019. On clicking it, a dropdown with the label \u201cShow estimated delivery time to\u201d appears. The dropdown has an option for Riyadh despite the fact that the description states that the product doesn\u2019t deliver to Riyadh. The user selected \u2018Riyadh\u2019 assuming it would show him estimated delivery date for Riyadh, but the dropdown disappeared without any feedback. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/AXa6kmLif5s\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">To avoid confusing the users and increasing their cognitive load, you should simplify dropdown menus by removing all unnecessary inputs. Do not include the user\u2019s current location in the \u2018Check Where It Ships\u201d dropdown menu and remove the estimated time label from the dropdown menu. Show the estimated time only if the user makes a selection.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>5. Checkout Process Issues\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">One of the users encountered several issues during the checkout process. The issues are listed below as they took place in order of the user journey. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Cluttered Notification Message on The Shopping Cart Page\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">After he selected a laptop, the user logged into his account and was directed to his cart which contained items he had previously added. A cluttered notification message appeared on the top of the page with a list of changes in prices and availability for each item. The message also stated that unavailable items had been moved to the \u201cSave for Later\u201d list. The user only glanced \u00a0at the message before closing it, assuming that it was something unimportant.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/gtHK09JDt4U\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Users who scan generally read headings, but do not read full text prose\u2013 this results in users missing critical information when a page contains dense text. The list in the notification message should be organized in a way that makes it easier for users to scan and understand. Clutter leads to a degradation of performance when trying to find certain information. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The use of meaningful labels, effective background colors, borders, and white space can reduce visual clutter. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Untitled List in Shipping Page\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>In the shipping page, there was a list of items on the bottom of the page without a title and no indication about what the list was for.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/wPTZKlqGBN8\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Clearly indicate what the list is for with a descriptive heading. Providing a descriptive heading allows users to readily understand the reason for having a list of items, and how the items relate to each other. The heading helps to inform users how items are categorized, or any prevailing principle or theme. Users are able to use lists better when they include headings.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Dead End in Shipping Page\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">In the shipping page, there was no \u201cNext\u201d or \u201cContinue\u201d button for the user to proceed. The user assumed it was because there was a problem with the address so he tried to change his address but it didn\u2019t work. Then he assumed that the dead end was because there were some unavailable items in his cart. So he began deleting items one by one.\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/zpAnZIFvoAo\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Make sure all your pages within the checkout process have \u201cContinue\u201d and \u201cBack\u201d buttons. Don\u2019t lead your users to dead ends. It is essential to provide the user with good navigational options. Dead ends create confusion and lead to additional and unnecessary clicks.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Browser Refreshes Each Time User Deletes An Item\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Every time he tried to delete an item from the shipping page, the page refreshed and took a long time to load. He wondered why there was no \u201cDelete All\u2019 button. To quicken the process, he opened his cart in a separate tab and started deleting items from there as well. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/7-JdtIfdGvA\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Don\u2019t refresh the page every time the user deletes something. Instead let the user make all the selections and then click a \u2018Filter\u2019 or \u2018Done\u2019 button so the page will only be refreshed after the user has finished making all selections. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">As a rule of thumb, if the result of the action will bring the user back to the same page, then eliminating refresh is a good idea. For example, instead of re-displaying the same account page after the user entered an incorrect password, giving feedback on the password immediately after entry can make for a nicer experience.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>User Was Not Sure Which Page He Was On\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">When he went back to the Cart page after deleting items, he was confused about which page he was on. There were lists for \u2018Save For Later\u2019 and \u2018Cart\u2019 and he said he wasn\u2019t sure which page it was. The laptop he deleted from the cart earlier was in the \u2018Save for Later\u2019 list and that confused him even further. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/TjEcV3Jhta0\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Provide feedback to let users know where they are in the Web site. Feedback provides users with the information they need to understand where they are within the Web site, and for proceeding to the next activity. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Examples of feedback include providing path and hierarchy information (i.e., \u2019breadcrumbs\u2019), matching link text to the destination page\u2019s heading, and creating URLs that relate to the user\u2019s location on the site. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Additionally, each web page should have a descriptive heading. Spending time during the design process to ensure that the site contains many carefully written headings and subheadings will allow users to rapidly understand which page they are on. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Points of Note\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Users preferred grid view rather than list view. Most users do not read a lot of text and prefer choosing products based on images. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Most users faced difficulties due to the shipping details &#8211; items that could not be shipped to Saudi were included in the product listing which frustrated users. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The checkout process on the Arabic UI had several issues with structure, organization, labeling and feedback. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">More rigorous usability engineering is required to ensure the fidelity of the solutions offered in this article.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>At UXBERT Labs we \\x3C/em>specialise\\x3Cem> in using scientific UCD processes to design world-class innovative and beautiful experiences. Hosting Saudi Arabia\u2019s first commercial and state-of-the-art Usability Lab, and a team of UX Researchers, Designers \\x3C/em>and\\x3Cem> Developers on-site in our Riyadh office, we deliver custom designed and built software that will help your businesses succeed, whatever the industry. If you have a project you\u2019d like to work with us on, get in touch at\\x3C/em> hello@uxbert.com\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},excerpt:$R[211]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>In the first of our Usability Engineering series, we ran some quick usability tests on e-services available through the Saudi Ministry of Interior\u2019s website. The aim was to show how even the simplest usability tests can bring to light significant usability flaws in your design. Moving away from government e-services, for our second article in [\u2026]\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},author:3,featured_media:11195,comment_status:\"closed\",ping_status:\"closed\",sticky:!1,template:\"\",format:\"standard\",meta:$R[212]={_acf_changed:!1,inline_featured_image:!1,_mi_skip_tracking:!1,footnotes:\"\",_vp_format_video_url:\"\",_vp_image_focal_point:$R[213]=[]},categories:$R[214]=[182,4,164,12],tags:$R[215]=[180,185,101,106,107,171],acf:$R[216]={main:\"/uploads/articles/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-e-commerce-website-souq-com/main.png\",sec:\"/uploads/articles/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-e-commerce-website-souq-com/sec.png\"},category_objects:$R[217]=[$R[218]={id:182,count:7,description:\"\",link:\"/category/customer-experience\",name:\"Customer Experience\",slug:\"customer-experience\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[219]=[]},$R[220]={id:4,count:3,description:\"\",link:\"/category/ecommerce\",name:\"Ecommerce\",slug:\"ecommerce\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[221]=[]},$R[100],$R[102]],tag_objects:$R[222]=[$R[223]={id:180,count:4,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/customer-experience\",name:\"customer experience\",slug:\"customer-experience\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[224]=[]},$R[225]={id:185,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/ecommerce\",name:\"ecommerce\",slug:\"ecommerce\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[226]=[]},$R[227]={id:101,count:8,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/usability-testing\",name:\"Usability Testing\",slug:\"usability-testing\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[228]=[]},$R[229]={id:106,count:9,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/user-research\",name:\"User Research\",slug:\"user-research\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[230]=[]},$R[133],$R[231]={id:171,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/web-design\",name:\"web design\",slug:\"web-design\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[232]=[]}]},$R[233]={id:10110,date:\"2017-09-27T14:31:15\",date_gmt:\"2017-09-27T14:31:15\",guid:$R[234]={rendered:\"/?p=10110\"},modified:\"2026-02-19T11:23:43\",modified_gmt:\"2026-02-19T11:23:43\",slug:\"why-ux-not-ui\",status:\"publish\",link:\"/why-ux-not-ui\",title:$R[235]={rendered:\"Why UX is Not UI\"},content:$R[236]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>User Experience (UX) Design\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\"> and \\x3C/span>\\x3Cb>User Interface (UI) Design\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\"> are some of the most misused terms in our industry.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Job advertisements often write \u201cUI/UX\u2019 as if they\u2019re interchangeable. As UX guru, \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.jnd.org/\\\">Don Norman\\x3C/a> said, \u201cthey just sort of entered the vocabulary and no longer have any special meaning. People use them often without having any idea why, what the word means, its origin, history, or what it\u2019s about.\u201d\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">So, what is the difference between UX and UI? What exactly is meant by \u2018good UX\u2019 or \u2018poor UI\u2019? \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">There\u2019s a lot of confusion surrounding these two terms. Let\u2019s first take a deeper look at what each of them means. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Defining UX and UI Design\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">User Experience or UX is the term used to encompass the entirety of how a person feels when interacting\u00a0with a system or product. While the system or product doesn&#8217;t necessarily have to be digital, in most modern contexts it generally refers to any product that has a form of human-computer interaction (HCI).\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The process of \\x3Cstrong>User Experience Design (UXD)\\x3C/strong> relates to how UX designers evaluate how a person feels when interfacing with a system and the methods and processes they follow to increase efficiency, satisfaction, utility and a range of other quality attributes.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-11126\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/UXBERT_UX_Process_v2.png\\\" alt=\\\"UXBERT_UX_Process_v2\\\" width=\\\"1558\\\" height=\\\"2048\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-10.png 1558w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-16.png 228w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-0.png 768w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-19.png 779w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-14.png 198w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-4.png 974w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-13.png 584w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-11.png 315w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-17.png 1280w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-7.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 1558px) 100vw, 1558px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The UX design process is a multifaceted one involving many steps ranging from user \\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">research and prototyping to development and usability testing. In short, designing\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">UX relates to designing\u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3Cb>the entire experience.\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Apart from research and design, a UX designer can also be\u00a0involved in areas like marketing, communications and support to ensure that the experience is cohesive from start to finish.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">UX design applies to the design of any medium &#8211; a product, a website, an app or an event. It draws from many disciplines like cognitive science, anthropology, psychology, computer science and graphic design.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>User Interface Design (UID)\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\u00a0on the other hand is concerned purely with how the product looks and feels. The user interface (UI) is the visual and technical elements of an interface, like the controls, the buttons etc that allow the user to interact with the product. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">To make things simpler, let&#8217;s consider UI in the context of a restaurant. The UI of a restaurant would be the visual theme around it &#8211; What color is the wallpaper? How are the chairs and tables aligned? How bright or dim is the lighting?\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">In the online world, a UI designer is responsible for creating typography, icons and other interactive elements that solve direct interaction problems for the user. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_10113\\\" style=\\\"width: 760px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-10113\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-10113\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2017/04/Screen-Shot-2017-04-12-at-2.57.59-PM.png\\\" alt=\\\"UI elements on the Facebook homepage\\\" width=\\\"750\\\" height=\\\"422\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-5.png 1056w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-9.png 300w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-8.png 768w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-20.png 1024w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-12.png 330w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-3.png 736w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-2.png 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 750px) 100vw, 750px\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-10113\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>UI elements on the Facebook homepage\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>The Difference between UX and UI \\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">For a lot of people, the line between UX and UI is blurry. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">To put it simply, UI is what faces the user when they look at the product whereas the UX covers\u00a0the entirety of the user&#8217;s dealings with the product. UI is generally about visual and information design around the interface. UX is about the complete experience, and it may not even be about an\u00a0interface. To deliver an exceptional overall experience, they must go hand in hand. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">To understand things easily, think of a rollercoaster in an amusement park. UX is the anticipation leading up to the ride. It is the feeling of excitement and thrill during the ride. It is the feeling of wanting to try it again.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">User Interface, or UI, is the way the ride is built. The construction material (nuts, bolts, concrete, steel), the quality of the ride (how sturdy is it?) and the placement of loops and turns.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Although the UI doesn\u2019t constitute the entirety of the experience of sitting in a ride, it is a very important part of it. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">But keep in mind that great UI does not mean great UX. And bad UI does not mean bad UX.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Some sites are designed to look pretty, but they can be frustrating to use because of slow loading times, frequent crashes or unconventional navigation.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">On the other hand, some sites have very poor text-based UI, but they have excellent UX because they do exactly what the users expect them to do.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Websites like \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://geo.craigslist.org/iso/us\\\">Craigslist\\x3C/a> and \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.ebay.com/\\\">eBay\\x3C/a> are examples of websites which have very simple text interface without fancy buttons. But they still have millions of users per day. Users like it because they can get things done easily and quickly.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv id=\\\"attachment_10112\\\" style=\\\"width: 699px\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption aligncenter\\\">\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" aria-describedby=\\\"caption-attachment-10112\\\" class=\\\"wp-image-10112 size-full\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2017/04/Craigslist-Step-2.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"Craigslist-Step-2\\\" width=\\\"689\\\" height=\\\"350\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-1.jpg 689w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-6.jpg 300w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-15.jpg 330w, /uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/inline-18.jpg 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 689px) 100vw, 689px\\\" />\\x3Cp id=\\\"caption-attachment-10112\\\" class=\\\"wp-caption-text\\\">\\x3Cem>Craigslist homepage\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">There are numerous factors that influence UX. Content, IA and interaction design are just three of the many aspects, aside from UI design, needed when creating a great user experience for your users. Each factor contains its own set of processes and requires a sense of empathy for the user. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">While visual aesthetics and the user interface design are essential in UX, it\u2019s important to remember that the user experience encompasses all aspects of your end-user\u2019s interaction with your site or product.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">At UXBERT Labs we specialize in UX Research, Design &amp; Development services that deliver world-class experiences. With offices in Riyadh and Dubai, our\u00a0team of UX Researchers, Designers, and Developers deliver custom designed and built software to\u00a0help businesses succeed.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>Interested in working with us? Email us at \\x3C/em>hello@uxbert.com\\x3Cem>\u00a0and let us know your research, design or development needs.\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},excerpt:$R[237]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>User Experience (UX) Design and User Interface (UI) Design are some of the most misused terms in our industry. Job advertisements often write \u201cUI/UX\u2019 as if they\u2019re interchangeable. But what is the difference between UX and UI? What exactly is meant by \u2018good UX\u2019 or \u2018poor UI\u2019? In this article we\u2019ll take a deeper look at what each of the terms mean.\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},author:3,featured_media:11088,comment_status:\"closed\",ping_status:\"closed\",sticky:!1,template:\"\",format:\"standard\",meta:$R[238]={_acf_changed:!1,inline_featured_image:!1,_mi_skip_tracking:!1,footnotes:\"\",_vp_format_video_url:\"\",_vp_image_focal_point:$R[239]=[]},categories:$R[240]=[3,148,12],tags:$R[241]=[165,103,107],acf:$R[242]={main:\"/uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/main.png\",sec:\"/uploads/articles/why-ux-not-ui/sec.png\"},category_objects:$R[243]=[$R[152],$R[172],$R[102]],tag_objects:$R[244]=[$R[179],$R[245]={id:103,count:11,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/userexperience\",name:\"User Experience\",slug:\"userexperience\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[246]=[]},$R[133]]},$R[247]={id:10768,date:\"2017-08-09T14:54:15\",date_gmt:\"2017-08-09T14:54:15\",guid:$R[248]={rendered:\"/?p=10768\"},modified:\"2025-09-01T08:15:52\",modified_gmt:\"2025-09-01T08:15:52\",slug:\"usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-ministry-of-interior\",status:\"publish\",link:\"/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-ministry-of-interior\",title:$R[249]={rendered:\"Usability Engineering in Saudi Arabia: Ministry of Interior E-services\"},content:$R[250]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>You are not your user.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Designing usable software, websites or mobile applications requires that you \\x3Cstrong>test designs with actual users\\x3C/strong>.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>At UXBERT Labs, UX Research is a key\u00a0part of our process. Whether it&#8217;s a brand new design, or a revamp of an existing one, interviewing users and running usability testing is essential. Starting projects with research means we&#8217;re\u00a0designing based on actual data rather than assumptions.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ca href=\\\"/5-reasons-business-needs-usability-testing/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">Usability testing\\x3C/a> shows you exactly where (and why) your customers have problems using your product. Companies that invest in usability testing see direct return on investment (ROI) through \\x3Ca href=\\\"/the-positive-roi-of-great-ux/\\\">increased sales and revenue, lower development cost and time, lower support costs, increased productivity and user satisfaction\\x3C/a>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>In this series, we&#8217;ll be conducting informal research sessions to demonstrate the kinds\u00a0of insights that can be collected from even the smallest amount of\u00a0research.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cblockquote>\\x3Cp>\\x3Cstrong>Usability testing shows you exactly where (and why) your customers have problems using your product.\\x3C/strong>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/blockquote>\\n\\x3Cp>Websites tested will include governmental services, e-commerce sites, business services and more.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Our\u00a0aim is to show\u00a0that regardless of whether a\u00a0site&#8217;s purpose is informational or transactional, \\x3Cstrong>testing with real users provides valuable insights, immediately\\x3C/strong>. And when these insights are fed back into the design process, it results in positive user experiences.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Similar to when we do formal\u00a0usability engineering\u00a0research, analysis of issues identified and suggested solutions are drawn from\u00a0a combination of \\x3Cstrong>scientific research-based usability guidelines\\x3C/strong> and an understanding of best practices\u00a0based on expert research experience.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>Eye-tracking technology is also used to support analysis of issues that revolve around layout, navigation and interaction.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Test Subject: Saudi Ministry of Interior Website\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>For our first set of sessions, we asked users to complete 2 simple tasks on the Saudi Ministry of Interior (\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.moi.gov.sa/wps/portal/Home/Home\\\">moi.gov.sa\\x3C/a>) website.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The Ministry of Interior website offers Saudi residents several e-services such as iqama renewal, driving license renewals, traffic violation enquiries and health insurance. It allows people to conveniently access these government services while aiming to minimise frustrations associated with the bureaucratic process.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>A total of 3 users took part in the tests, each of whom were asked to use the site to perform the following tasks:\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\u2022 Check for any unpaid traffic violations\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\u2022 Check to see if their fingerprint scan had been successfully registered and updated in the system\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Ideal User Journey For Task Success\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>The following is a list of the steps the user should ideally take to navigate to task success in the quickest, most efficient way.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>\\x3Cb>Task 1:\u00a0\\x3C/b>\\x3C/em>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Checking For Unpaid Violations\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 1\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Hover over\u00a0&#8220;Electronic Inquiries&#8221; label in the global navigation menu on the Homepage\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 2\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Hover over \u201cTraffic\u201d in the\u00a0&#8220;Electronic Inquiries&#8221; drop down\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 3\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Click on\u00a0&#8220;Public Query Traffic Violation&#8221;\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 4\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Enter personal details and Image Code\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 5\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Click \u2018View\u2019\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/g0nal-Kwm_w\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>\\x3Cb>Task 2:\u00a0\\x3C/b>\\x3C/em>\\x3Cb>\\x3Ci>Checking If Fingerprint Is Updated\\x3C/i>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 1\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">:\u00a0\\x3Ci>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Hover over\u00a0&#8220;Electronic Inquiries&#8221; label in the global navigation menu on the Homepage\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 2\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">:\u00a0\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Hover over \u201cPassports\u201d\u00a0in the\u00a0&#8220;Electronic Inquiries&#8221; drop down\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 3:\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\u00a0Click on\u00a0Public Query FingerPrint Enrollment \\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 4\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Enter your ID Number and Image Code\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Step 5\\x3C/span>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">: Click \u2018View\u2019\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/9086cF66oHA\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch2>\\x3Cb>Issues and Recommendations\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h2>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Reporting of identified issues begins with a single usability fault that was identified across both tasks.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">It&#8217;s followed by issues listed in the order with which they occurred as users went through the journey.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Common Issue: Missed Label &#8220;Electronic Inquiries&#8221; On the Main Navigational Menu\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Cp>When asked to check for their traffic violations or fingerprint status, almost all users went straight to the E-services label.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Users typically get an idea of the menu contents from tab\u00a0labels. However, in this instance, users clicked through\u00a0each category and reviewed items in the submenus one by one to figure out where they could find the traffic violations and fingerprint options.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>When users compared the \u2018E-Services\u2019 and \u2018Electronic Inquiries\u2019 menu options, they couldn\u2019t understand the difference between the submenus for each category because there was a lot of redundancy in the available options.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">This issue points to potential issues with\u00a0the website\u2019s labeling and information architecture. Bad information architecture causes the majority of outright user failures (\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Nielsen, 2004\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">).\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/FZjf4PTa0oM\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Potential\u00a0solution for this type of issue are\u00a0twofold:\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ci>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Make menu labels more descriptive\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Tab labels should be \\x3Cstrong>clearly descriptive of their function or destination\\x3C/strong>\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">. Tabs should be descriptive enough to allow users to know what it contains and to minimize selection errors. When tab labels cannot be made clear because of the lack of space, include descriptive tips when the user hovers over a specific menu.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Redesign the website\u2019s information architecture\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Serious issues with the website&#8217;s site map and navigation need more than just descriptive labels. Designers need to present information in a structure that \\x3Cstrong>reflects user needs and the site\u2019s goals\\x3C/strong>. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Good information architecture enables users to understand the nature of the site\u2019s organizational relationships, supports them in locating information efficiently and reduces the chances of users boredom or frustration. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Improving the structure of the website so that it is more intuitive for users can be done with the help of organizing techniques like card sorting. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Card sorting can help you figure out the optimal menu structure by telling you where people would want to find something\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">. It\u2019s a reliable method for finding patterns in how and where users would expect to find content or functionality. It can also help you figure about appropriate nomenclature for your menu labels. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Task 1\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Issue 1: Arabic Error Message in English UI\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">When the user logged into his account to check the unpaid traffic violations, he got an error message after entering his personal details. The error message was in Arabic so the user couldn\u2019t understand what went wrong. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/rmdwSSK4EPQ\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">It&#8217;s necessary to ensure consistency of elements through the website. Language inconsistencies not only confuse your users, they also reduce their trust in your brand, degrade website credibility and convey an overall lack of attention to detail\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Research suggests that \\x3Cstrong>the number of errors made using visually inconsistent displays is reliably higher\\x3C/strong> than when using visually consistent displays. Tasks performed on more consistent interfaces have reduced task completion times, fewer errors, more user satisfaction and reduced learning time\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Issue 2: Inconsistent UI When Users are Logged In\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">There is an inconsistency between the interfaces when users are logged in and when they are not. When users are logged in, the \u2018Public Query for Traffic Violations\u2019 option is available under both E-services and Electronic Inquiries. If the user isn\u2019t logged in, it\u2019s not available under E-services. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/_7hZ65y-OSw\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal\\x3C/b>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">:\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Information across the website should be organized consistently to allow users\u00a0to perform tasks in the same sequence and manner across similar conditions. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cstrong>Consistency is acknowledged as an essential design principle\\x3C/strong> and should be applied throughout the content and interactions within the website\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Task 2\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>\\x3Cb>Issue 1: Fingerprint Status Message at the Top Was Not Noticeable\\x3C/b>\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">When the user clicked \u2018View\u2019 after entering his personal details, he did not notice the fingerprint status message at the top. Because of this, he entered the \u2018Image Code\u2019 for verification twice because he thought the page refreshed. The user had change blindness, a phenomenon where users do not always notice the changes that occur on the screen. This change blindness affects critical information and leads to user confusion and task failure.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/uMdmQC3PTZI\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal:\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Users can easily overlook changes in state because the visual presentation blends in with the rest of the page. Slow feedback, long load times, and extraneous things competing for your user\u2019s attention can all make them blind to seemingly obvious changes on the page.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">One way to deal with change blindness is to minimize visual interruptions by avoiding page reloads where possible. When page reloads are necessary, keep attention focused on the action button location while the page loads, then display any state changes in the same general area. \\x3Cstrong>The ideal solution is to transform the button into a progress indicator. \\x3C/strong>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Another technique to deal with this issue is to strategically place important new visual elements close to the point where the user&#8217;s\u2019 eyes are already focused. Participants in change blindness studies are better at detecting changes in the center of the visual field, where most of their attention is focused. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Using appropriate visual emphasis such as contrast, size, and padding can ensure that important elements are noticeable\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Ch4>\\x3Cb>Issue 2: Users Thought The \u201cPassport\u201d Label Was Unclear\\x3C/b>\\x3C/h4>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The user did not expect to find the fingerprint query under \u201cPassports\u201d. It wasn\u2019t immediately obvious to him that he could check his fingerprint status from \u2018Passports\u2019. He expected to find it under the \u2018Expats\u2019 category. He mentioned that the Arabic word \u201cAl Jawazat\u201d made more sense than the English label \u201cPassports\u201d. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cdiv class=\\\"video-container\\\">\\x3Ciframe loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/yXN19Tz6o58\\\" width=\\\"560\\\" height=\\\"315\\\" frameborder=\\\"0\\\" allowfullscreen=\\\"allowfullscreen\\\">\\x3C/iframe>\\x3C/div>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Proposal: \\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">This kind of issue arises when you rely on direct translation from Arabic to English content or vice versa. When creating your website copy, you\u2019ll need to make an effort to think about \\x3Cstrong>what exactly it is you want to communicate and how best to do it by taking into account the context of the users. \\x3C/strong>\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">A key component of a website\u2019s information architecture is an effective nomenclature or labeling system\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">A number of IA tools can be used to determine effective nomenclature and organization for the website including but not limited to card sorting and content inventories. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cb>Points of Note\\x3C/b>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cul>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Most of the website\u2019s issues revolved around issues with site information architecture. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Several labels were unclear to users because of which users struggled to find information. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The website had language inconsistencies (Arabic Error Message in English UI) and inconsistencies between the interfaces when the users were logged in and when they were not. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3Cli style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Because of potential issues with the\u00a0website\u2019s information architecture, there is a need for card sorting usability engineering to redesign it.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/li>\\n\\x3C/ul>\\n\\x3Cp>&nbsp;\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>At UXBERT Labs we \\x3C/em>specialise\\x3Cem> in using scientific UCD processes to design world-class innovative and beautiful experiences. Hosting Saudi Arabia\u2019s first commercial and state-of-the-art Usability Lab, and a team CX Researchers, Designers and Developers on-site in our Riyadh office, we&#8217;ll help you create customer experiences that make your business stand out and keep your customers coming back. If you have a project you\u2019d like to work with us on, get in touch at\\x3C/em>\u00a0\\x3Ca class=\\\"theme\\\" href=\\\"mailto:hello@uxbert.com\\\">hello@uxbert.com\\x3C/a>\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},excerpt:$R[251]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>Usability testing shows you exactly where (and why) your customers have problems using your product. Companies that invest in usability testing see direct return on investment (ROI) through increased sales and revenue, lower development cost and time, lower support costs, increased productivity and user satisfaction.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>In this series, we\u2019ll be conducting so called \u201cproto-research sessions\u201d to demonstrate the kinds of insights that can be collected from even informal research.\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},author:3,featured_media:10816,comment_status:\"closed\",ping_status:\"closed\",sticky:!1,template:\"\",format:\"standard\",meta:$R[252]={_acf_changed:!1,inline_featured_image:!1,_mi_skip_tracking:!1,footnotes:\"\",_vp_format_video_url:\"\",_vp_image_focal_point:$R[253]=[]},categories:$R[254]=[2,182,164,12],tags:$R[255]=[172,89,103,106,107,200],acf:$R[256]={main:\"/uploads/articles/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-ministry-of-interior/main.png\",sec:\"/uploads/articles/usability-engineering-saudi-arabia-ministry-of-interior/sec.png\"},category_objects:$R[257]=[$R[258]={id:2,count:3,description:\"\",link:\"/category/case-studies\",name:\"Case Studies\",slug:\"case-studies\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[259]=[]},$R[218],$R[100],$R[102]],tag_objects:$R[260]=[$R[261]={id:172,count:2,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/eye-tracking\",name:\"eye tracking\",slug:\"eye-tracking\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[262]=[]},$R[263]={id:89,count:5,description:\"\",link:\"/tag/saudi-arabia\",name:\"saudi arabia\",slug:\"saudi-arabia\",taxonomy:\"post_tag\",meta:$R[264]=[]},$R[245],$R[229],$R[133],$R[157]]},$R[265]={id:10736,date:\"2017-07-31T14:27:26\",date_gmt:\"2017-07-31T14:27:26\",guid:$R[266]={rendered:\"/?p=10736\"},modified:\"2025-09-01T08:18:17\",modified_gmt:\"2025-09-01T08:18:17\",slug:\"july-meetup-recap-emotional-design\",status:\"publish\",link:\"/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design\",title:$R[267]={rendered:\"July Meetup Recap: Emotional Design\"},content:$R[268]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Our all-female July meetup was another exciting evening full of stimulating discussions! \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Attendees included both local and international developers, designers and students who were curious about emotional design and it\u2019s importance in creating great user experiences. They all looked forward to the opportunity to connect with people with a similar passion for UX. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Our very own UX superstar Linah Aburahmah was the speaker for the event. She kicked off the meetup by asking people to introduce themselves while helping themselves to the snacks and drinks provided. This sparked off a discussion about everyone\u2019s respective backgrounds and what attracted them to this event. By the end of the ice-breaker, there was a relaxed and intimate atmosphere around the room. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The presentation began with an entertaining example of a company who successfully incorporated emotionally intelligent design in their website. Linah showed the audience some quotes outlining the company\u2019s design philosophy and asked them to guess which company it was. The audience gave answers like \u2018Google\u2019 or \u2018Apple\u2019 but were surprised that the answer was actually Mint.com.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">With\u00a0people\u2019s attention was engaged by\u00a0an entertaining introduction, Linah dove\u00a0into the key concepts and principles of usability and user experience. The crowd kept the discussion going with their own hilarious personal experiences on websites with poor usability. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">After a quick run-down of the basics of UX, Linah went on to explain how beautiful products induce positive emotions and these emotions directly impact the product\u2019s usability! She used several studies and examples from real-world websites to demonstrate this to the audience. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The fact that aesthetics and usability were related was a new concept for many audience members. The rest of the presentation was aimed at helping the audience understand the different theories of emotional design and how to implement them in design. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cimg loading=\\\"lazy\\\" decoding=\\\"async\\\" class=\\\"aligncenter size-full wp-image-10737\\\" src=\\\"/wp-content/uploads/2017/07/IMG_6615.jpg\\\" alt=\\\"IMG_6615\\\" width=\\\"5184\\\" height=\\\"3456\\\" srcset=\\\"/uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-8.jpg 5184w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-7.jpg 300w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-3.jpg 768w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-11.jpg 1024w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-1.jpg 150w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-10.jpg 330w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-9.jpg 900w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-12.jpg 1800w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-5.jpg 736w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-0.jpg 1920w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-4.jpg 621w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-2.jpg 1280w, /uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/inline-6.jpg 414w\\\" sizes=\\\"(max-width: 5184px) 100vw, 5184px\\\" />\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">While talking about the theories of emotional design, the audience had a discussion about how a product\u2019s brand identity can help a person develop an emotional connection to it. They cited the iPhone as an example and said that most people buy Apple products because the brand has a \u2018hip\u2019 or \u2018cool\u2019 vibe. Our cognitive scientist and comedienne in-residence, Nadeen Alloush, gave her own example; saying, \u201cThis is why I always buy water that says \u201cCanadian Water\u201d on the bottle. I know it\u2019s just plain water but I can\u2019t help it! I\u2019m Canadian! When I see it, I just need to have it!\u201d \u00a0\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">The most exciting part of the meetup was the Twitter challenge at the end of the presentation. Audience members were asked to explain their attachment to Snapchat using emotional design concepts. They were given 10 minutes to tweet \\x3C/span>\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://twitter.com/uxbert\\\">\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">@uxbert\\x3C/span>\\x3C/a>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\"> their answers to the question.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">Although we had awesome answers from everyone in the crowd, Sara (@Sarasization)\\x3C/span>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">\u00a0had the most innovative and original idea and was awarded the first prize. She described the cultural reasons behind why Snapchat was especially popular in Saudi Arabia. The prize gift was the book called \u201cEmotional Design\u201d by Don Norman. \\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cblockquote class=\\\"twitter-tweet\\\" data-lang=\\\"en\\\">\\n\\x3Cp dir=\\\"ltr\\\" lang=\\\"en\\\">\\x3Ca href=\\\"https://twitter.com/uxbert\\\">@uxbert\\x3C/a>\\x3Cbr />\\nSnapchat&#8217;s simplicity, basic functionality, and relation to cultural aspects (privacy) creates a perfect combination. ??\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\u2014 Sara Alshim (@Sarasization) \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://twitter.com/Sarasization/status/889905206678495237\\\">July 25, 2017\\x3C/a>\\x3C/p>\\x3C/blockquote>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cscript src=\\\"//platform.twitter.com/widgets.js\\\" async=\\\"\\\" charset=\\\"utf-8\\\">\\x3C/script>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>The crowd was very pleased with the entire experience and all of them were willing to return for future meetups. The ladies stated that the UXBERT Labs office had a really cool interior and requested a tour before leaving. By the end of the meetup we had all gone from strangers with similar interests to friends exchanging our personal experiences together.\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">We\u2019re always proud to bring together the UX community and share our expertise together and learn from each other. Make sure you don\u2019t miss out on future meetups by joining our User Experience &amp; Design group on \\x3Ca href=\\\"https://www.meetup.com/User-Experience-Design-Riyadh-UX/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" rel=\\\"noopener\\\">meetup.com\\x3C/a> or signing up to our blog email newsletter.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">We\u2019re looking forward to seeing more and more UX enthusiasts in our upcoming meetups!\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">For any questions, comments or suggestions feel free to email us at [hello@uxbert.com]\\x3C/span>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Ci>\\x3Cspan style=\\\"font-weight: 400;\\\">At UXBERT Labs we specialize in UX Research, Design &amp; Development services that deliver world-class experiences. With offices in Dubai, Riyadh and London, our\u00a0team of UX Researchers, Designers, and Developers deliver custom designed and built software to\u00a0help businesses succeed.\\x3C/span>\\x3C/i>\\x3C/p>\\n\\x3Cp>\\x3Cem>Interested in working with us? Email us at \\x3C/em>hello@uxbert.com\\x3Cem>\u00a0and let us know your research, design or development needs.\\x3C/em>\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},excerpt:$R[269]={rendered:\"\\x3Cp>Our all-female July meetup was an exciting evening with local and international developers, designers and students joining. In this recap article we do a quick run down of how the evening went.\\x3C/p>\\n\",protected:!1},author:3,featured_media:10742,comment_status:\"closed\",ping_status:\"closed\",sticky:!1,template:\"\",format:\"standard\",meta:$R[270]={_acf_changed:!1,inline_featured_image:!1,_mi_skip_tracking:!1,footnotes:\"\",_vp_format_video_url:\"\",_vp_image_focal_point:$R[271]=[]},categories:$R[272]=[148,12,14],tags:$R[273]=[199,103,107,171],acf:$R[274]={main:\"/uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/main.png\",sec:\"/uploads/articles/july-meetup-recap-emotional-design/sec.png\"},category_objects:$R[275]=[$R[172],$R[102],$R[276]={id:14,count:5,description:\"\",link:\"/category/ux-design-meetup\",name:\"UX &amp; Design Meetup\",slug:\"ux-design-meetup\",taxonomy:\"category\",parent:0,meta:$R[277]=[]}],tag_objects:$R[278]=[$R[197],$R[245],$R[133],$R[231]]}]},ssr:!0}],lastMatchId:\"/en/choosing-the-right-ux-design-consulting-firm-in-saudi-arabia-a-practical-guide-for-startups-and-enterprises\",dehydratedData:void 0})($R[\"tsr\"]);if (typeof $_TSR !== 'undefined') $_TSR.c()</script><meta charSet=\"utf-8\"/><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1,minimum-scale=1,maximum-scale=5\"/><script async=\"\" src=\"/replica/analytics/gtag/js?id=G-B8Z56ZXQE9\"></script><title>Choosing the Right UX Design Consulting Firm in Saudi Arabia: A Practical Guide for Startups and Enterprises</title><meta http-equiv=\"Cache-control\" content=\"public\"/><meta name=\"robots\" content=\"all\"/><meta name=\"format-detection\" content=\"telephone=no\"/><meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-08-11T12:40:38.915Z\"/><meta property=\"og:updated_time\" content=\"2026-08-11T12:40:38.915Z\"/><meta name=\"author\" content=\"UXBERT Labs\"/><meta property=\"og:image:width\" content=\"1200\"/><meta property=\"og:image:height\" content=\"630\"/><meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#0b042a\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-TileColor\" content=\"#FFFFFF\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-TileImage\" content=\"mstile-144x144.png\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-square70x70logo\" content=\"mstile-70x70.png\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-square150x150logo\" content=\"mstile-150x150.png\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-wide310x150logo\" content=\"mstile-310x150.png\"/><meta name=\"msapplication-square310x310logo\" content=\"mstile-310x310.png\"/><meta name=\"application-name\" content=\"\u00a0\"/><meta name=\"description\" content=\"User experienc